Context: E598 is Euler's reply to Lagrange's 'Recherches d'arithmétique' (reduced binary quadratic forms). The worked example on the preceding pages is the form p^2 - 30q^2, with divisor modulus 4n = 120.

Top of p.306 (end of Cor. 3, §39). The eight admissible residues a mod 120, taken up to sign, are 1, 7, 13, 17, 19, 29, 37, 49. Each gets a character. Character 1 means the prime itself is represented by ±(x^2 - 30y^2) (D=F). Character 2 means its double is represented, i.e. it is represented by ±(2x^2 - 15y^2) (2D=F). The assignments are 1,2,2,2,1,1,2,1:
- 1 = 1^2;
- 7 = -(2·4 - 15);
- 13 = -(2 - 15);
- 17 = 2·16 - 15;
- 19 = 49 - 30;
- 29 = -(1 - 30);
- 37 comes from 10t^2 - 3, which Euler has merged into class 2 (Cor. 2);
- 49 = 7^2.
Euler remarks that both characters occur equally often (4 and 4). In modern terms, discriminant 120 has narrow class number 4: the fundamental unit 11+2√30 has norm +1, so ±forms are inequivalent in the narrow sense. Allowing ±a passes to wide classes, of which there are 2: the principal form x^2-30y^2 and 2x^2-15y^2. The two characters therefore split the kernel subgroup of the character mod 120 into two cosets of equal size, one per class.

Scholion §40. Euler contrasts his rule (primes p ≡ α mod 4n divide some p^2 ± nq^2) with Lagrange's method of listing primes form by form, which he calls laborious. He wishes for a proof of his assertion, which is essentially quadratic reciprocity. He then lists 'momenta' toward a proof.
- (1) The residues prime to 4n split into two classes, α (divisors) and 𝔄 (never divisors).
- (2) The α-values form a closed system containing all squares and closed under products and powers, comprising exactly half of the reduced residues mod 4n. In modern terms: an index-2 subgroup of (Z/4nZ)*, the kernel of the Kronecker character (∓n/·).
- (3) The two classes differ essentially.
- (4) No 𝔄-number, nor any multiple of one, divides p^2 ± nq^2 with (p,q)=1.
- (5) A product of two 𝔄-numbers lies in the α-class: the quotient group has order 2. Hence many composite α-numbers (those with an 𝔄-factor) are not divisors.
- (6) (Next page.) Delete those composites. It is 'maxime probabile' that all remaining numbers, in particular all primes ≡ α, are divisors. This is a heuristic, not a proof.

The overall purpose is to show that his residue-class description is the efficient and complete characterization of prime divisors of quadratic forms, and to reduce everything to one theorem to be proved.
